`,s.jsx(g,{items:[{q:"Which score function needs no extra learnable parameters, and what's the trade-off?",a:"Dot-product scoring — it's a plain inner product of the decoder and encoder hidden states. The trade-off is that it requires both vectors to have the same dimensionality, since a dot product is undefined otherwise; general and additive scoring both relax that requirement at the cost of extra learned weights."},{q:"Why does scaling by 1/√d matter for dot-product attention as dimensionality grows?",a:"A dot product of two roughly-independent, unit-variance vectors of dimension d has variance that grows linearly with d, so its typical magnitude grows like √d. Without correcting for that, scores at high dimensions spread out wide enough that softmax saturates toward a near one-hot output, losing the soft weighting attention is supposed to provide. Dividing by √d keeps the score scale roughly constant as d changes."}]}),`
